A||N||6||Verticalspeed||Left Front, Right Rear And Steering SHIMMY||||5||
Z||000000||Verticalspeed||12-24-2000||02:35 AM||||Everything is fine up to 65 MPH freeway speeds. Going from 65-80MPH causes a shimmy that appears to be coming from the left front wheel on to the right rear. I also get a bit of shimmy on the steering wheel itself.<BR>I am suspecting the following:<P>1. out of round tires<BR>2. alignment maybe slightly off<BR>3. Bad rims<BR>4. Tie rod ends?<BR>5. Bushings?<P>It only occurs at high speeds. Any recomendations. Does anyone have the alignment specs for an 86 SE?||||reg||5
Z||000001||KRMFiero||12-24-2000||08:48 AM||||Welcome to the forum!    <BR>try getting it High-speed balanced, also cheak and make sure you cant remove the lugs nuts with your fingers (heheh that happined to my old escort)<P>Kyle <P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<BR>Front - AudioBahn 4 x 10<BR>Headrest - AudioBahn 3 1/2<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-24-2000||09:19 AM||||Check the tires for loose or missing weights. If you lose a wheel weight or the wheels were not balanced to begin with, This can also cause the problem. Was the car sitting for a while? If it was the tires could have "flat spots". Tire air pressure could be something else to check.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||12-24-2000||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Verticalspeed:<BR><B>Everything is fine up to 65 MPH freeway speeds. Going from 65-80MPH causes a shimmy that appears to be coming from the left front wheel on to the right rear. I also get a bit of shimmy on the steering wheel itself.<BR>I am suspecting the following:<P>1. out of round tires<BR>2. alignment maybe slightly off<BR>3. Bad rims<BR>4. Tie rod ends?<BR>5. Bushings?<P>It only occurs at high speeds. Any recomendations. Does anyone have the alignment specs for an 86 SE?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Missing wheel weights usually cause a pretty heavy and rapid vibration. From what you describe, your problem sounds different.<BR>     Jack up the front of the car, grasp a front wheel at the 12 & 6 o'clock positions and shake it by pulling with one hand and pushing with the other. If you feel ANY play, it is too much. To figure out where it's from you may need to lie under the car and watch while someone else shakes the wheel.<BR>     Shaking with your hands at the 12 & 6 positions checks for loose ball joints and/or wheel bearings. Shaking it with your hands at the 3 & 9 positions checks for loose inner/outer tie rod ends and/or wheel bearings. If your wheel bearing needs adjustment it will show up as play in both positions.<BR>     On a high mileage fiero, any play you feel is likely to be from multiple sources. I replaced all four ball joints on my '87 GT and it still needs a right inner tie-rod end and left wheel bearing ajustment.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Verticalspeed||12-24-2000||01:04 PM|||| <BR>1. Car was sitting for a while<BR>2. Not sure if the wheels have ever been balanced - bought it this last summer<BR>3. Will go to discount tire and them "high speed" balance the wheels, front and back.<BR>I noticed no "weights" on any of the wheels. Big clue here?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||12-25-2000||07:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Verticalspeed:<BR><B> <BR>1. Car was sitting for a while<BR>2. Not sure if the wheels have ever been balanced - bought it this last summer<BR>3. Will go to discount tire and them "high speed" balance the wheels, front and back.<BR>I noticed no "weights" on any of the wheels. Big clue here?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'd say that no visible weights on the wheels means that balancing is a part of your problem. There is no special procedure for "high speed" balancing: just go in and ask to have all four wheels balanced. It should cost between $5 and $10 per wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Verticalspeed||12-25-2000||07:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> I'd say that no visible weights on the wheels  should cost between $5 and $10 per wheel.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B>Thank you.</B> Actually I pulled both wheels off  and they <B>DID</B> have weights on them but the scary part was that the lug nuts weren't in very tight. I retorqued them with a torque wrench,(100ftlbs), went for a drive and the ride was much better but still somewhat squirly at high speeds. I got some crosswinds that kept swaying my car. Kind of scary. SO, I pulled the wheels off again.<BR>The sway bar bushings, then ones on the frame, were cracked and weak just by their appearance. I also noticed that the end link bushings had lots of hairline cracks on them. I was able to compress them by hand! <BR>I am replacing all the bushings with poly and rebalancing both front wheels. Next, I will do a 4 wheel alignment if things don't improve. <B>My question to you guys then is: what are the effects of old, worn sway bar bushings?</B><BR>P.S.- I have a set of New monroe gas shocks in front - <P>||||reg||5
